{\pard\plain \s0\ql\sb60\sa60\f0\fs22\ql\sb50\sa50\cbpat11 {\f11\fs14\cf4 1. ITT or All Enrolled analysis set may be used instead of Safety for studies that use the ITT or All Enrolled analysis set as the primary analysis set. For multiphase studies, [Study Specific] analysis set may be used based on the study design.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 2. The following conventions will be used for the imputation of date of birth when it is partially missing or not collected:}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4   - If only month and year of birth is collected, then "15" will be imputed as the day of birth}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4   - If only year of birth is collected, then "01 July" will be imputed as the day and month of birth}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4   - If year of birth is missing, then date of birth will not be imputed.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4   In general, age collected at Day 1 (in years \u123?or specify unit\u125?) will be used for analyses and presented in listings. If age at Day 1 is not available for a participant, then age derived based on date of birth and the Day 1 visit date will be used instead. If an enrolled participant was not dosed with any study drug, the randomization [enrollment] date will be used instead of the Day 1 visit date.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4   For screen failures, the date the first informed consent was signed will be used for the age derivation. Age required for longitudinal and temporal calculations and analyses (e.g., estimates of creatinine clearance, age at date of AE) will be based on age derived from date of birth and the date of the measurement or event, unless otherwise specified. (Note, see SAP and discuss with Biometrics team).}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 3. Baseline characteristics (e.g., weight, height, and BMI) are presented in the same table with the demographics.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 4. Height, weight, and/or body mass index (BMI) may also be displayed by sex if specified in the SAP. Derived BMI values will be reported instead of the collected value from eCRF.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 5. Optional row for missing or unknown category may be added where applicable (e.g., interim analyses) and dropped for final analysis if all zero counts.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 6. Categories for race and ethnicity should follow the order in the study eCRF.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 7. As a default, categories with all zeros are omitted. If they add value, all categories as listed on the CRF may be displayed, including categories with all zeros.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 8. Missing values may be included in percent calculations so percentages should add to 100%.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 9. *Use TRTxxA(N) when displaying the safety analysis set, and TRTxxP(N) otherwise.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 10. The values of this variable cannot be used directly to satisfy the requirements of the table. An additional variable should be created in the table program to change to case or associate a format so that the display matches the mockup display.}\line {\f11\fs14\cf4 11. Categories for age group and BMI group may be added if specified in the SAP. Up to a maximum of three age groups is permitted in the display.}\par}
